dence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "North Carolina sits near the center of the presidential map, with a partisan lean that favors Republicans by about a point. The term-limited incumbent leaves both parties fielding new nominees, so neither side starts with an incumbency advantage. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or market pricing are available yet, and neither campaign has reported fundraising, leaving fundamentals as the only guide.\n\nThe race therefore rates as a genuine tossup with a slight Republican tilt. Recent headlines concern the parties' 2028 nomination contests rather than the state's general election, including a CPAC straw poll win for JD Vance among Republicans. Until candidates, polling, and spending take shape, the state's underlying lean is the best available evidence, and it points to a margin of roughly a point.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4508, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4639, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4659, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.4636, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.4637, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.4628, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.4642, "margin": -1.0}]}